1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,933
Three facts make this book almost
impossible to dismiss.

2
00:00:03,933 --> 00:00:07,978
First: its parchment was made more than six
centuries ago.

3
00:00:07,978 --> 00:00:12,410
Second: at least five different people
appear to have written it.

4
00:00:12,410 --> 00:00:16,343
Third: after generations of cryptographers,
linguists, historians,

5
00:00:16,343 --> 00:00:19,612
and computer scientists, no one can read a
single page with

6
00:00:19,612 --> 00:00:23,268
scholarly agreement. Inside Yale's Beinecke
Library, the object is

7
00:00:23,268 --> 00:00:27,090
cataloged simply as Manuscript 408. Its two
hundred thirty-four pages

8
00:00:27,090 --> 00:00:31,024
contain impossible plants, rotating skies,
human figures, and thousands

9
00:00:31,024 --> 00:00:34,237
of words in an alphabet found nowhere else.

10
00:00:34,237 --> 00:00:37,284
If it is a cipher, the key has vanished.

11
00:00:37,284 --> 00:00:40,830
If it is a language, every known speaker is
gone.

12
00:00:40,830 --> 00:00:44,652
And if it is a hoax, someone invested
extraordinary time, skill,

13
00:00:44,652 --> 00:00:47,478
and money to create a lie that has survived
for six

14
00:00:47,478 --> 00:00:51,300
hundred years. This is not the story of a
solved code.

15
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:54,957
It is the evidence behind the book no one
can read.

16
00:00:54,957 --> 00:00:57,827
Begin with the object, not the theories.

17
00:00:57,827 --> 00:01:01,167
The Voynich Manuscript is a small parchment
codex, about nine by

18
00:01:01,167 --> 00:01:04,821
six inches. Radiocarbon analysis of four
samples dated its animal-skin

19
00:01:04,821 --> 00:01:07,221
pages to between 1404 and 1438.

20
00:01:07,221 --> 00:01:11,031
Its inks and pigments use materials
compatible with historical manuscript

21
00:01:11,031 --> 00:01:14,789
production. Important distinction:
radiocarbon dates the parchment,

22
00:01:14,789 --> 00:01:17,607
not each stroke of ink. By itself, it
cannot prove the

23
00:01:17,607 --> 00:01:20,060
exact day the writing was added.

24
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:23,818
But together with the book's physical
construction, pigments, wear,

25
00:01:23,818 --> 00:01:27,471
and later ownership record, the evidence
strongly supports a genuinely

26
00:01:27,471 --> 00:01:30,811
medieval codex. Then paleographer Lisa
Fagin Davis examined tiny

27
00:01:30,811 --> 00:01:35,195
differences in the handwriting: how glyphs
lean, connect, and repeat.

28
00:01:35,195 --> 00:01:38,275
Her conclusion was not one scribe, but
five.

29
00:01:38,275 --> 00:01:41,041
That changes the scale of the mystery.

30
00:01:41,041 --> 00:01:44,694
Several people seem to have learned the
same unfamiliar writing system

31
00:01:44,694 --> 00:01:47,617
and coordinated a large illustrated book.

32
00:01:47,617 --> 00:01:50,331
We still do not know what they wrote.

33
00:01:50,331 --> 00:01:52,992
But we know this was organized work.

34
00:01:52,992 --> 00:01:57,531
Open the manuscript and it divides into six
visual worlds.

35
00:01:57,531 --> 00:02:00,950
The largest section resembles an herbal.

36
00:02:00,950 --> 00:02:04,866
Each page presents a plant, but many roots,
leaves, and flowers

37
00:02:04,866 --> 00:02:09,280
resist reliable identification. Next come
astronomical and astrological

38
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:13,321
pages: suns, stars, zodiac-like rings, and
circular diagrams that

39
00:02:13,321 --> 00:02:17,548
almost resemble calendars. A third section
shows small human figures

40
00:02:17,548 --> 00:02:21,154
linked by pools, pipes, and flowing shapes.

41
00:02:21,154 --> 00:02:25,568
Curators call it biological or
balneological—connected with bathing—but

42
00:02:25,568 --> 00:02:29,608
that label describes the imagery, not its
meaning.

43
00:02:29,608 --> 00:02:33,960
Cosmological foldouts expand into elaborate
medallions.

44
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:38,809
Pharmaceutical pages pair plant parts with
vessel-like objects.

45
00:02:38,809 --> 00:02:42,601
The final pages look like short recipes,
each entry marked by

46
00:02:42,601 --> 00:02:47,947
a star-shaped flower. Every image feels one
step away from recognition.

47
00:02:47,947 --> 00:02:51,740
That is the trap. A plant can look
medicinal without matching

48
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:55,780
a species. A circle can look astronomical
without mapping a known

49
00:02:55,780 --> 00:02:59,759
sky. Because the labels cannot be read,
pictures cannot securely

50
00:02:59,759 --> 00:03:04,981
explain the words—and the words cannot
securely explain the pictures.

51
00:03:04,981 --> 00:03:08,600
The ownership trail begins in imperial
Prague.

52
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:12,872
Yale identifies Holy Roman Emperor Rudolf
the Second as the manuscript's

53
00:03:12,872 --> 00:03:16,847
first confirmed owner. A surviving account
says he paid six hundred

54
00:03:16,847 --> 00:03:20,229
gold ducats and believed the work might be
by the English

55
00:03:20,229 --> 00:03:24,144
thinker Roger Bacon. Modern scholarship
treats that attribution as

56
00:03:24,144 --> 00:03:28,179
speculation. The book later passed through
the library of pharmacist

57
00:03:28,179 --> 00:03:32,925
Jacobus de Tepenec and then to Prague
alchemist Georgius Baresch.

58
00:03:32,925 --> 00:03:36,841
Baresch called it a riddle and asked the
celebrated Jesuit scholar

59
00:03:36,841 --> 00:03:40,638
Athanasius Kircher for help. In 1666,
Johannes Marcus Marci sent

60
00:03:40,638 --> 00:03:44,376
the manuscript to Kircher in Rome with a
letter still preserved

61
00:03:44,376 --> 00:03:48,173
inside it. Then, silence. For more than two
centuries, the trail

62
00:03:48,173 --> 00:03:52,207
becomes uncertain. In 1912, Polish-American
rare-book dealer Wilfrid

63
00:03:52,207 --> 00:03:56,242
Voynich purchased it from a Jesuit college
near Rome.

64
00:03:56,242 --> 00:04:00,573
The mystery took his name. After passing
through later owners, bookseller

65
00:04:00,573 --> 00:04:03,776
H. P. Kraus donated it to Yale in 1969.

66
00:04:03,776 --> 00:04:08,226
Today every page is online. Access is no
longer the problem.

67
00:04:08,226 --> 00:04:10,184
Interpretation is.

68
00:04:10,184 --> 00:04:13,940
Look closely and the writing is not random
scribbling.

69
00:04:13,940 --> 00:04:16,336
It runs mainly left to right.

70
00:04:16,336 --> 00:04:20,038
Glyphs form repeated word-like units. Some
forms favor the beginning

71
00:04:20,038 --> 00:04:23,740
of a line; others cluster near certain
illustrations.

72
00:04:23,740 --> 00:04:27,061
Neighboring words can differ by only one
sign.

73
00:04:27,061 --> 00:04:31,036
Researchers have tested frequency,
repetition, entropy, and relationships

74
00:04:31,036 --> 00:04:34,574
between sections. Some results resemble
patterns found in natural

75
00:04:34,574 --> 00:04:39,093
language. That sounds like a
breakthrough—until the caution arrives.

76
00:04:39,093 --> 00:04:42,305
A cipher can preserve statistical
structure.

77
00:04:42,305 --> 00:04:44,810
A lost shorthand can create it.

78
00:04:44,810 --> 00:04:48,403
A specially designed notation system can
create it.

79
00:04:48,403 --> 00:04:52,323
Even an algorithm for generating
meaningless text can produce convincing

80
00:04:52,323 --> 00:04:56,515
repetition. And because some glyphs are
ambiguous, changing the transcription

81
00:04:56,515 --> 00:05:00,272
can change the statistics. Linguists Claire
Bowern and Luke Lindemann

82
00:05:00,272 --> 00:05:03,974
reviewed this evidence and reached a
careful conclusion: computation

83
00:05:03,974 --> 00:05:07,295
can eliminate weak ideas and reveal
structure, but it has not

84
00:05:07,295 --> 00:05:10,833
established one accepted language or
decipherment.

85
00:05:10,833 --> 00:05:13,719
Patterns prove that there is a system.

86
00:05:13,719 --> 00:05:16,877
They do not yet prove what the system says.

87
00:05:16,877 --> 00:05:20,284
Four broad explanations remain in
contention.

88
00:05:20,284 --> 00:05:24,544
Theory one: an ordinary language hidden by
substitution, abbreviation,

89
00:05:24,544 --> 00:05:28,462
or a complex cipher. The regularity
fits—but no proposed key produces

90
00:05:28,462 --> 00:05:31,984
a coherent reading across the whole
manuscript.

91
00:05:31,984 --> 00:05:35,675
Theory two: a lost shorthand or invented
alphabet.

92
00:05:35,675 --> 00:05:39,310
This could explain why the text looks
linguistic while resisting

93
00:05:39,310 --> 00:05:43,172
familiar scripts. Without a parallel text,
it is extremely difficult

94
00:05:43,172 --> 00:05:46,807
to test. Theory three: a constructed
notation meaningful only to

95
00:05:46,807 --> 00:05:51,009
a small community—perhaps medical,
religious, or scholarly.

96
00:05:51,009 --> 00:05:54,758
Five scribal hands make a shared convention
plausible, but they do

97
00:05:54,758 --> 00:05:58,733
not reveal its purpose. Theory four: an
elaborate hoax.

98
00:05:58,733 --> 00:06:02,481
Experiments show that rule-based nonsense
can imitate some Voynich

99
00:06:02,481 --> 00:06:06,627
statistics. But “meaningless” does not
explain the costly parchment,

100
00:06:06,627 --> 00:06:10,830
sustained artwork, multiple writers, or the
intended buyer.

101
00:06:10,830 --> 00:06:14,862
A hoax would still be a remarkable
organized production.

102
00:06:14,862 --> 00:06:18,895
Each theory explains one clue and collides
with another.

103
00:06:18,895 --> 00:06:22,529
Cipher explains secrecy, but not the
missing key.

104
00:06:22,529 --> 00:06:25,994
Language explains structure, but not
identity.

105
00:06:25,994 --> 00:06:28,947
Hoax explains failure—but not effort.

106
00:06:28,947 --> 00:06:32,406
Artificial intelligence has now joined the
hunt, comparing glyph

107
00:06:32,406 --> 00:06:36,027
sequences and candidate languages at
enormous scale.

108
00:06:36,027 --> 00:06:39,431
It can find similarities no person would
notice.

109
00:06:39,431 --> 00:06:43,377
It can also force a dramatic answer from a
bad assumption.

110
00:06:43,377 --> 00:06:46,835
A real solution needs more than a viral
headline.

111
00:06:46,835 --> 00:06:50,294
The method must be reproducible. The
grammar and vocabulary must

112
00:06:50,294 --> 00:06:54,185
make historical sense. And the same key
must produce consistent readings

113
00:06:54,185 --> 00:06:58,130
across botanical pages, circular diagrams,
and plain text.

114
00:06:58,130 --> 00:07:02,724
No public claim has yet crossed that
threshold to scholarly consensus.

115
00:07:02,724 --> 00:07:06,723
Perhaps the missing key survives in an
uncataloged archive.

116
00:07:06,723 --> 00:07:10,777
Perhaps this is a writing system with no
surviving relative.

117
00:07:10,777 --> 00:07:14,181
Or perhaps the manuscript was designed to
create the feeling of

118
00:07:14,181 --> 00:07:17,964
meaning without ever surrendering it. Here
is what the evidence allows

119
00:07:17,964 --> 00:07:21,585
us to say: the Voynich Manuscript is a
genuine medieval codex,

120
00:07:21,585 --> 00:07:25,260
produced by multiple hands, filled with an
unidentified script whose

121
00:07:25,260 --> 00:07:29,259
meaning remains unresolved. The pages are
open to everyone.

122
00:07:29,259 --> 00:07:32,934
The answer is not. This is RAHASYA: Global
Mysteries.

123
00:07:32,934 --> 00:07:36,177
Which theory best fits the evidence—and
what does it fail to

124
00:07:36,177 --> 00:07:37,420
explain?
